{
 "$comment": "Known failures of the serialize->parse round-trip property `ce.parse(t.latex).isSame(t)` (structural tier) over docs/mathnet/parser-test-cases.json. Maintained by docs/mathnet/scripts/check-roundtrip.ts (npm run check:roundtrip); regenerate with `npm run check:roundtrip -- --update`. `reason` is `bug` (a serializer/round-trip defect to fix) or `documented-lossy` (a form the serializer is contractually allowed to lose). `class` groups identical-cause entries; see `classes` below. CI fails only on failures NOT listed here.",
 "date": "2026-08-04",
 "engineVersion": "0.100.3",
 "lastChecked": "2026-08-05",
 "classes": {
  "continuation-placeholder-multiply-regroups": {
   "reason": "bug",
   "note": "A canonical Multiply whose operands include ContinuationPlaceholder serializes with MIXED separators - invisible juxtaposition between the literal factors, explicit \\times around the ellipsis (`ab\\times\\dots\\times z`). On reparse the juxtaposed run binds tighter and becomes a nested Multiply. Repro: ce.parse('a\\\\cdot b\\\\cdot\\\\dots\\\\cdot z') -> 'ab\\\\times\\\\dots\\\\times z' -> ['Multiply',['Multiply','a','b'],'ContinuationPlaceholder','z']. The Add form ('a+b+\\\\dots+z') round-trips, so this is Multiply-specific."
  },
  "continuation-placeholder-fraction-aggregation": {
   "reason": "bug",
   "note": "A Multiply of Rationals containing a ContinuationPlaceholder is aggregated into one \\frac, moving numerators and denominators across the ellipsis: Multiply(3/2, 6/5, ContinuationPlaceholder, X) serializes as \\frac{3\\times6\\times\\dots\\times X}{2\\times5}, which reparses with 3*6/(2*5) folded to 9/5."
  },
  "fraction-fallback-inverse-power": {
   "reason": "bug",
   "note": "When the denominator itself contains a fraction (or an ellipsis), the serializer declines \\frac and falls back to `(a)(b)^{-1}`, which reparses as Multiply(a, Divide(1,b)) rather than Divide(a,b). Repro: ce.parse('\\\\frac{100}{\\\\sin(\\\\frac{5\\\\pi}{9})}') -> '(100)(\\\\sin(\\\\frac{5\\\\pi}{9}))^{-1}'. Note the numerator-1 case is safe: '\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sin(\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2})}' -> '(\\\\sin(...))^{-1}' reparses as Divide(1,...)."
  },
  "quotient-factor-merged-into-fraction": {
   "reason": "bug",
   "note": "A Multiply with a Divide/reciprocal factor is emitted as ONE fraction over the whole product, so the reparse is a Divide of the product instead of a Multiply. Repros: '\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\cdot\\\\frac{1}{a+x}' -> '\\\\frac{1}{2(a+x)}'; '(-1)^{m}\\\\frac{1}{a}' -> '\\\\frac{(-1)^{m}}{a}'. CANDIDATE documented-lossy: the rewrite is value-preserving prettification; it fails only the structural tier. Needs a maintainer ruling before it is either fixed or reclassified."
  },
  "singleton-tuple-dropped": {
   "reason": "bug",
   "note": "A one-operand Tuple serializes as `(x)`, which reparses as plain `x` - the tuple-ness is lost. Repro: ce.parse('x=1,') -> ['Tuple',['Equal','x',1]] -> '(x=1)' -> ['Equal','x',1]. (Trailing commas in the corpus are the usual source of singleton Tuples.)"
  },
  "operator-symbol-serializes-empty": {
   "reason": "bug",
   "note": "An operator name used as a VALUE serializes to the empty string, silently deleting the operand. Repro: ce.parse('(A,+)') -> ['Tuple','A','Add'] -> '(A,)' -> ['Tuple','A']. Same for Multiply/\\\\cdot. Worst class in the list: the LaTeX round trip loses data outright."
  },
  "at-over-declared-set-base": {
   "reason": "bug",
   "note": "At over a set-typed base reserializes to a bracket form that no longer type-checks. Repro: ce.parse('\\\\mathbb{Z}_n') -> ['At','Integers','n'] -> '\\\\Z[n]' -> ['At',['Error',['ErrorCode',\"'incompatible-type'\",\"'dictionary | indexed_collection'\",\"'set<finite_integer>'\"]],'n']. The first parse arguably should not have produced At over Integers either."
  },
  "complex-i-vs-imaginary-unit": {
   "reason": "bug",
   "note": "The parser maps the literal `i` to ['Complex',0,1] but `\\\\imaginaryI` to the symbol 'ImaginaryUnit'; the serializer emits `\\\\imaginaryI` for both. Repro: ce.parse('i') -> ['Complex',0,1] -> '\\\\imaginaryI' -> 'ImaginaryUnit'. Two canonical spellings of the same value; canonicalization should pick one."
  },
  "prime-serialization-not-delimited": {
   "reason": "bug",
   "note": "Prime serializes as `A^\\\\prime` with no brace/space, so an immediately following letter is swallowed into the command name: `\\\\angle BA'C` -> `\\\\angle BA^\\\\primeC` -> Error 'unexpected-command' on '\\\\primeC'. `A'` in isolation round-trips; the defect needs a letter directly after the prime."
  },
  "angle-assign-lhs-not-reparsable": {
   "reason": "bug",
   "note": "`\\\\varangle Z Y X := ...` boxes as Assign('Angle', Function(...)) and reserializes with a parenthesized argument list on the LHS (`\\\\angle(Z, Y, X)\\\\coloneq...`), which the Assign parselet rejects with 'expected-a-symbol'."
  },
  "forall-body-not-delimited": {
   "reason": "bug",
   "note": "`\\\\forall <cond>, <body>` is serialized with NO delimiters around the body, so on reparse the body is truncated at the first lower-precedence operator and the rest reassociates above the ForAll. Repro: ce.parse('a_0=9 \\\\text{ and } a_1=3 \\\\text{ for all } k \\\\ge 0') -> ForAll(k>=0, And(a_0=9, a_1=3)) -> '\\\\forall k\\\\ge0, a_0=9\\\\land a_1=3' -> And(a_1=3, ForAll(k>=0, a_0=9))."
  },
  "forall-held-body-not-canonicalized": {
   "reason": "bug",
   "note": "A Tuple in a ForAll body reserializes as `(a,b,c)` and comes back as Delimiter(Sequence(...)) instead of Tuple - the held/lazy body is never canonicalized on the reparse route. Repro: ce.parse('a_0=1, a_1=3 \\\\text{ for all } n \\\\ge 1') -> ForAll(n>=1, Tuple(...)) -> '\\\\forall n\\\\ge1, (a_0=1,a_1=3)' -> ForAll(n>=1, Delimiter(Sequence(...))). The same corpus rows also retain HorizontalSpacing (\\\\quad) inside the body."
  },
  "builtin-head-vs-value-def-drift": {
   "reason": "bug",
   "note": "A name that is BOTH a library operator and used as a free variable binds inconsistently WITHIN one parse: the head-position occurrence takes the builtin operator definition, a later occurrence auto-declares a value definition. The reparse sees the value definition everywhere, so the trees differ by binding identity with IDENTICAL MathJSON. Repro: ce.parse('N, N+1') - N types as '(any, integer?) -> unknown' in pass 1, 'number' in pass 2. Same for D (derivative) in '(DB+BC)^2=AD^2+AC^2.'."
  },
  "inference-drift-canonical-order": {
   "reason": "bug",
   "note": "Pass 1 leaves inferred free-symbol types in the engine and pass 2 canonicalizes differently under them. Repro: ce.parse('2f(ab)') on a fresh engine has .ops [2, f, Multiply(a,b)] - a NESTED, unflattened Multiply, f typed 'unknown' - while the reparse of '2abf' has .ops [2,a,b,f], flat. Two defects behind one entry: (i) canonical Multiply left a nested Multiply unflattened; (ii) .json prints ['Multiply',2,'a','b','f'] for BOTH trees, so MathJSON equality is not a proxy for isSame. The same-engine reparse is forced (cross-engine isSame is false for every symbol), so this class cannot be designed away by the harness."
  },
  "exact-numeric-fold-gap": {
   "reason": "bug",
   "note": "Pass 1 leaves an exact numeric product unfolded where pass 2 folds it. Repro: ce.parse('n \\\\equiv 0, 4, 5 (mod 6)') -> ['Multiply',5,6,'d','m','o'] in pass 1, ['Multiply',30,'d','m','o'] after the round trip. Isolated '5\\\\times 6 d' folds correctly, so the gap is context-dependent."
  }
 },
